[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known, with one fix queued, and closed on 5 October. The
reports merged into it do not all describe the same bug. Run the desk's own cause logic over the
export and they split, and each part needed a fix of its own.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The engineering update on #4471 answers two of these three groups: the parser is fixed, and the
hint is won't fix because booking-v3 is retired. It says nothing about the third. Those five
reports were counted into the ticket and closed with it, and nobody has reproduced one.
</Callout>

## parser · the shared parser reads every date month first

Every tenant on file writes the day before the month, so a date they type as 12/05 comes back as
December. This is the bug #4471 names, and `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` in the
engineering update is the fix for it. Fixed.

## hint · booking-v3 prints the order its tenants do not use

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Its tenants write the day first,
their people type what the hint tells them, and the form then reads it the tenant's way. The date
flips for the opposite reason, so a fix to the parser does not touch it. Only form reports from
booking-v3 tenants land here, which is why this group is small and steady rather than loud.

The engineering update closes this one as won't fix: booking-v3 is retired, so the form that
printed the hint is gone. That ends the reports. It does not make the group part of the parser
bug, and 33 people were told for six months that their problem was known and being fixed.

## one-day move · the invite lands a day out, not a month out

These reports do not describe a flipped month. A month-first parse cannot turn Tuesday 12 May
into Monday 11 May. One reporter had a client in Lisbon get Tuesday and a client in Toronto get
Monday for the same meeting, which points at time zones and not at date order. The cause logic
places a report by tenant and channel, so it cannot see this at all: it files these under
`parser` with everything else from the same tenants. The desk noticed them and wrote them down
in `desk-notes.json`, and there the matter stopped.

## Why they arrived as one ticket

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges a report into an open ticket when the
subject line matches, and nobody reads the body. Three bugs that make a date look wrong produce
the same few subject lines, so the desk merged all of them into the first ticket that had one.
Intake's own tag was no help either: the `intake tagged them` column above is what intake guessed
from the reporter's browser, and it disagrees with the cause inside every group.

This is what the ticket's own page shows today, with its own component:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The row it marks as not this ticket has had no ticket of its own since April, and the footnote
under it is the one-day move.

## What to do

- One ticket per problem, each counted on its own, in the
  [public repo](https://github.com/arahagikoa/mega/issues) this page was written from. The parser
  ticket is closed as fixed and the hint ticket as won't fix, which is what the update says.
  **The one-day move ticket stays open**, because #4471 closing did not answer it.
- Reproduce one of the five. Two of them point at time zones, which is a different kind of bug
  and may still be live on a product that now parses dates correctly.
- Decision 0001 needs a replacement record. Matching on a subject line cannot separate two bugs
  that produce the same subject, and this ticket is what that costs: a group with no ticket for
  six months, and five reports closed without an answer.

